namespace OpenTPW;

/// <summary>
/// Register of the original-save import mappings that are not traced (docs/reverse/SAVE-plan.md §8.2,
/// docs/SAVE-CONTAINER.md, "Import into OpenTPW"). Each entry has a matching <c>// [APPROX:SAVE-NNN]</c>
/// comment at its code site. Values the import drops are also reported in <see cref="OriginalEconomyImport.Evidence"/>.
/// </summary>
public static class SaveApproximations
{
	public static readonly IReadOnlyList<(string Id, string Assumption, string EvidenceNeeded)> All = new[]
	{
		("SAVE-002", "a saved guest using an attraction (16) or held by a guard (17) is not imported (the riders sit in the ride script's walk, bounce and head tables, which the import does not restore, DET-013; a held guest needs its escorting guard, whose state 18 the staff import restores idle), and a guest in a queue (states 11–14, or its idle interlude, state 8 with mSavedState 11) is imported only when its attraction's saved queue (mFirstInQ, mQNext, mQPrev, mPersonBeingLoaded) reads as the loader's chain of guests of that ride; a queue head imported as handed to the script (state 14) whose script does not hold it in VAR_LETMEON after the script import (the script import refused, or no readable RSSE block) is imported called but not admitted (state 13 at the stand point), where tp.exe's test 0x4E0A70 sends it straight into state 16, which needs the walk, bounce and head tables; a guard escorting a guest is imported idle and one chasing a guest the import does not restore gives the chase up at his first update; an imported guest in an activity (state 8) decides again at once, one at the booth (4) that was not yet weighed waits OpenTPW's booth time (ARRIVAL-006), a turn stamp before the save's turn counts from the import, and the saved state (mSavedState) outside the queue interlude, the admission flag (mBeenAdmitted) of a guest that is not in state 11 or in its interlude, and the 32-slot event ring (AddEvent 0x50C100) are not imported. No corpus or Easymode save holds a guest in states 8/11-15 or a set mFirstInQ; the queue import is checked on synthetic saves only.", "the ride script's walk, bounce and head tables for the riders (16) and the escorting guard's state for a held guest (17)"),
		("SAVE-006", "a claim stamp after the save's turn is clamped to it where the original would hold the claim longer, and a claimant thing slot the original reuses for a later thing could keep a claim alive, which OpenTPW's negative claimant word does not; a player's own save's park gate gives the park its name (its two sign lines) and an idle attraction saved with its open word clear stays closed, the shipped Easymode seed keeps the gate's default lines in the player's language (they are the same text); the other attractions' sign lines are their OBJECT_NAMES row, and the raw links (mesh instance, script and track handles, flags, footprint, entrance and exit cells, upgrade parent, balloon sprite) are rebuilt by the placement and the queue (cells, ends) from the laid queue cells (the head, the links and the called guest are the saved ones, GuestSimulation.ImportOriginal)", "whether a player-named non-gate attraction exists (its sign lines 0x75B5EC would then differ from the OBJECT_NAMES row) and the raw links a loaded attraction keeps (the staff claims are imported as saved: an attraction's claimant word and turn, 0x004E01F0, lapsing lazily, 0x004E0220)"),
		("SAVE-007", "original-save lab projects of objects the level archives do not define, and current project identities absent from their category's reconstructed table, are dropped and reported; known current projects are kept as saved even when the picker would not offer them (ParkResearch.SelectAsSaved), and none of the 11 pinned corpus labs contains an unknown project", "original continuation with missing object/project identities, or an explicit supported-save scope decision for those cases; the ordinary corpus does not establish that boundary"),
		("SAVE-010", "the advisor presentation block (its width is traced, SaveBlockWidths), the second clock word (the advisor clock, applied when the automatic advisor attaches; the first, the game clock the saved scripts' waits count from, is applied with their run state, SavedScriptImport), vanilla time, game-system words, the cheat chunk's second byte (+0xB of the cheat object 0xFB3520, which only its constructor and the loader 0x405570 write; the first, the cheat mode, is imported, docs/CHEATS.md), advisor scoring and UI record count (SaveRuntimeBlocks), the weather, park analyser apart from its recent-research rings, monthly statistics and the arrival ring and object and hire stamps the automatic advisor takes when it attaches, tag system, mechanic HQ and UI message receiver things, the world state, the arrival vehicle handles and bytes (the saved scripts of the vehicles resume on their own, but the arrival scheduler's words that point at them are not imported, ARRIVAL-001) and the calendar session start, and the advisor controller's time signature, sound, three raw words, mRequestedLastTutorial and mMessagePlaying are not imported (its current record is imported as the last tutorial advice when an automatic advisor attaches), nor are its pending advice and history when no automatic advisor is attached; of the Sound block (loader 0x51C400) a park started from an original save (a player's own or the shipped Easymode seed) applies its master, effects, music and speech volumes and on-flags to the mixer until the options take over, as the original's sound tick 0x51BD70 does (GameAudio.ApplySavedSound; the options file is not changed); its sound flags, voice handles, crowd, rain and reverb words (recomputed within frames, as in the original), its dirty, speech, duck and listener-freeze words and the sound library's voice list behind three listener floats the loader discards (writer 0x6B6C30, reader 0x6B6DA0; not QMixer data) are tp.exe's own engine state, which OpenTPW's audio does not share ([EXT:original-engine-state-dumps]); the camera is imported (zoom, yaw and the required point of interest, loader 0x42CEC0, a level starts its camera there) without the loader's yaw correction by the word 0x78D912; of the cell map the path cells, occupancy, MAP attributes, each path cell's texture and turn and the litter (type, collector and claim turn, with the handyman jobs on it) are read, and a saved path tile is drawn only while the cell's path neighbours equal the saved ones and its turn is 0/90/180/270 (otherwise the tile is chosen from the neighbours)", "how the world state and the arrival words (the current arrival vehicle's handle and the arrival bytes) steer the original's arrival update beyond what OpenTPW's arrival scheduler rebuilds, the camera loader's yaw correction word 0x78D912, and the original's reconstruction of the weather, park analyser apart from its recent-research rings, monthly statistics, arrival ring and object and hire stamps, tag system, mechanic HQ and message receiver things (no restore of their own was found)"),
		("SAVE-011", "a Coasters block with a coaster whose type the caller does not give (a payload split without the park's coaster types, or a station that is no single saved attraction of a World the readers measure) is located by its delimiter's bytes (every other block ends where its reader stops, SaveBlockWidths, the World included since the loader 0x5179C0 is traced, then its tag must follow, like tp.exe's loader 0x415270; the park import passes the coaster types, so its Coasters block ends where its reader stops, SaveCoasterBlock.TryMeasure; a Theme Park World payload's World that does not read as the original's, a synthetic payload, falls back to its delimiter too; the Theme Park Inc profile has no such fallback and reads its World exactly): a payload that splits in more than one way there, or holds the COAS delimiter's bytes more than MaximumDelimiterOccurrences times, is rejected, while the original loader runs each block's own reader and then compares the next 4 bytes with the block's tag (the tags at 0x415371 and its successors), never searching, and loads such payloads", "a caller-independent source of the coaster types' train slot and car counts (type +0x2E8 and +0x2F4) for the tools that split a payload without the park's object catalogue"),
		("SAVE-012", "Restart Park reloads OpenTPW's own snapshot (opentpw-<theme>-restart.json, the park-resume format), not the original's <save>\\restart.INTS: the original writes that file in state 15 of the park state machine 0x54E360 through 0x550B30 (skipped while the GameType word 0xFB3B7C is 1, the online visit) and reloads it for a restart request (code 1 from the confirm callbacks 0x48B5B0 and 0x48B620 through 0x5508B0: state 10 to 13, which only advances, and 14, where 0x5996D0(1) ends the running park and 0x5AC5F0 loads the file through 0x414D40, then back to 10) and takes no new snapshot after that reload; OpenTPW restarts into a park-resume snapshot instead of an original save file (which snapshot is kept is the design choice [EXT:restart-snapshot-per-start]; the camera block that is not restored belongs to SAVE-010)", "whether OpenTPW's park-resume snapshot restores everything the original's restart.INTS load does: an original-format writer exists now (the save function 0x414920, reached through 0x5AC780, and the payload writer 0x4164C0 are reproduced byte for byte, and OriginalParkExport writes the economy, the research lab and the object controls' researched bytes onto a template of the level) but a restart snapshot of a park with placed objects, built paths, staff or guests cannot be written in the original's format until those are exported (their attraction bodies, scene records and cell fields), so Restart Park cannot use an original restart.INTS yet (SAVE-010 lists what the import leaves out)"),
		("SAVE-013", "the Scripts block (SaveScriptsBlock: the sprite-script slot table and each 0x118-byte VM record, tp.exe 0x475730) holds one sprite script per saved guest or staff member (the person's script handle, created by 0x4D4140 from the program table 0x75A418: entry 0x5A is the standing loop that holds frame 0, which the staff drop 0x505EA0 sets as program 3, entry 0x2A the walking one; each park save of the corpus holds one script per saved guest) and one per litter item (0x4D93B0, the programs 0x74F050 to 0x74F168 with the litter type); its records are read exactly and not applied: OpenTPW's guests and staff animate from their state and clock and its litter sprites from the cell (the programs are threaded code, handler pointers with inline operands: 0x476940 sets a frame, 0x476020 jumps), so the saved program counters, wake ticks (+0x7C) and periods have no counterpart; the Particles block's pool is applied to the level's particle system (SaveParticleState maps it partially: it drops the emitter hold, group, screen-space, ring and immortal words, the bounds, the effector group, mode and strength, the pool capacity, density and low-detail flag and the saved template tables, so modified templates 101 to 104 restore as the file's; the corpus loses nothing), and the post-load pass 0x5200B0 hides the screen-space emitters (the saved Button sparkles), leaving Jungle's WaterFall; the effector slot stores no template id, which SaveParticleState recovers from template bytes the tick does not write by direct displacement (writes through a base register were not checked; no corpus save has a live effector)", "whether the saved animation phase of a guest or litter sprite (frame, wake tick, period) shows anything OpenTPW's state-derived frames lack, and a save with a live effector to confirm the template match"),
		("SAVE-920", "the riders of an imported coaster's trains are not restored: each car's two guest lists (car +0x28/+0x2C, the riders on the lap and the guests seated at the station) and the train's rider count (+0x10) are read and dropped, because the guests riding or seated (state 16) are not imported (SAVE-002). Everything else of a saved coaster is restored as tp.exe's loader 0x437B60 does it (OriginalCoasterImport): the bind to the station by its mesh instance id (a park whose block binds to no station, where the original crashes at 0x437CD5, is refused), the station nodes, every pylon node with its loop pair, flags, loft, yaw, bank, incline and loop rotate, the end node, the cut, the state word, power factor and seats, the trains in service at their saved positions, speeds and flags through the first rebuild (0x43C450 keeps them while flag 0x4000 is set), and the station script's speed word through the script loop's setter (0x43B2B0), which lays the trains out again when it differs from 60; the decoded block is verified against tp.exe's reader and synthetic blocks, not against a real Theme Park World save with a coaster, which none of the local saves holds", "the riding-guest import (state 16), which the train rider lists then join"),
		("SAVE-921", "the coaster header's flag word is kept raw: the saver (tp.exe 0x437300) writes controller +0x3C bits 0 and 1 as wire 0x1 and 0x2 (the track is complete, the circuit is cut open: SaveCoaster.TrackComplete and CircuitOpen) and the track flags (+0) 0x20 to 0x800 as wire 0x4 to 0x100 (0x43743C..0x4374D0), and the loader maps them back (0x4380D4..0x438335); the section flag word is decoded (SaveCoasterSectionFlags: wire 0x1/0x2/0x4 and the cut, link and intersection marks are restored, wire 0x8/0x10/0x20 are the loop and zero-bank flags the node constructor derives again); of the track flags only 0x800 has a traced meaning (the trains are set up: set by 0x43BE23 with the train slots, cleared by 0x435814 when they are removed, COAST-plan §22); a scan of tp.exe's coaster module (0x432000..0x44C000) for the tests, sets and clears of controller +0 finds no user of 0x20, 0x40, 0x80, 0x100, 0x200 or 0x400 apart from the saver and the loader (the other bits it finds are 0x800, 0x2000, 0x4000 and the replayer's 0x8000), so they look like dead data only the file carries (0x40 is no outside mode: that is the mode bits of the state word, header +4), but the scan covers immediate tests and masks on the controller pointer, not every way the word could be read", "a complete data-flow proof that nothing outside the saver and the loader reads the controller's track flags 0x20, 0x40, 0x80, 0x100, 0x200 and 0x400 (controller +0)"),
		("SAVE-940", "the names of the Theme Park Inc attraction's 19 history rings (ThemeParkIncAttractionRing) are descriptions of what the game adds to each ring, read from the code that increments it (the history object 0x5ea020, the end-of-visit step 0x6118d0, the guest outcome step 0x612740 and the price test 0x6120c0), not names the loader's log gives: the order, capacities, periods (a new-day message 11 and a new-month message 12 from the calendar step 0x6083d0) and the money, customer, walk-away, mood-grade and price rings are traced, but DailyRatedVisits, DailyDeclinedGuests and DailyNeedChecksFailed stand for several or only partly identified conditions (the info word +0x21c and the guest thresholds 0xa01b1c..0xa01b44 are unnamed; the ThirstEffect and HungerEffect words at +0x200 and +0x204 are named by their position among the info keys and by use), and the prebuilt's rings are all empty, so no ring value has been seen in a save", "a Theme Park Inc save whose attractions have run, and the info field +0x21c and the thresholds 0xa01b1c..0xa01b44"),
		("SAVE-944", "the Theme Park Inc guest (model 1), staff (4 to 8) and gardener (20) bodies have no witnessed save: their layouts (a guest is 619 bytes; a staff member 684 to 696 bytes plus 4 for each saved entry of its six history rings, at most 30 each) are read from the load routines of Game.exe (guest 0x60d260, staff base 0x6267c0 and the role loads, ring 0x62f2b0) and checked only against an emulation of those routines, never against a save's bytes. For all traced bodies (also the staff HQ, advisor controller, park analyser, research lab, weather and challenge system, whose prebuilt widths the layouts reproduce) a word keeps its stored width because the loaders copy each member, so a field's signedness or float type is not known except where a user of the member shows it (traced: the guest's seven meters are floats, six of them named from the needs step 0x619c0e and the outcome step 0x612740; a staff member's three meters are floats whose names are not assigned; the weather's wind and last lightning location are floats), and the meaning of the staff, analyser and guest rings, of the enumerations (weather, challenge, advisor, state words) and of the other words is not traced; a ring count above its capacity (30, 3, 144, 50 or 5 entries; the byte ring is 50, its 51st slot being padding, so the original overruns only from entry 52) is refused, although the original would read on, and a count with the sign bit set is refused where the original compares signed and reads no entry. Guests, staff and gardeners have never been checked against a real save, and a layout mistake now refuses the whole save where the old header search recovered", "a Theme Park Inc save with guests, staff or a gardener to check the layouts on real bytes, and the users of the remaining words (staff meter names, the enumerations, the analyser and challenge words) to type them"),
	};
}
